WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:32.667
Write at the owner. A booking write reaches the current primary. Its acknowledgment policy determines what the application can assume about durability and replication at that moment.

2
00:00:32.667 --> 00:01:07.667
Ship changes. Changes travel to replicas. With asynchronous replication, the primary can reply before all copies have applied the latest write.

3
00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:56.417
Read a lagging copy. A confirmation read reaches Replica A before the booking arrives there. The user may see an older state even though the primary accepted the write.

4
00:01:56.417 --> 00:02:34.333
Choose a consistent path. Use the primary or an explicitly supported consistency mechanism for confirmation. Public catalog reads can have a different staleness allowance.

5
00:02:34.333 --> 00:03:08.333
Coordinate failover. If the primary fails, promotion needs a clear election or orchestration process and fencing. Avoid letting an isolated old writer accept conflicting new changes.

6
00:03:08.333 --> 00:03:51.083
Keep recovery separate. Replication also copies many mistakes. Maintain tested backups and recovery points independently rather than counting live copies as protection against every kind of data loss.
